Switch: Aurora's sqlite caches without a journal file

On the console both caches (dawn_cache.db and pipeline_cache.db on the SD card) failed
their first transaction with "database disk image is malformed" on every run, even
after the files were deleted, so every shader and pipeline was compiled again each run.

On Horizon the caches use journal_mode=MEMORY and synchronous=OFF instead of WAL
(compiled out there) and NORMAL: no rollback journal file is created, stat'ed or
unlinked next to the database, and nothing waits for the card. A cache that cannot be
opened, for any reason, is removed and created once more from an empty file (the shared
patch native/patches/aurora/0007 does the removal and retry, for damaged files only on
other platforms). The sqlite build adds exclusive locking and in-memory temp files, and
its error log goes to the run log (switch/aurora/sqlite_horizon.c).

Switch only (switch/native/aurora/patches); not for upstream.

diff --git a/lib/sqlite_utils.hpp b/lib/sqlite_utils.hpp
index 9370469..a6e51b2 100644
--- a/lib/sqlite_utils.hpp
+++ b/lib/sqlite_utils.hpp
@@ -14,8 +14,14 @@ namespace aurora::sqlite {
 inline int apply_cache_pragmas(sqlite3* db, const std::string& path, Module& log) {
   (void)path;
   (void)log;
+#if defined(__SWITCH__)
+  // Horizon (the Switch's SD card through newlib): no rollback journal file next to the database
+  // (a damaged file is removed and rebuilt, see is_damaged) and no syncs. WAL is compiled out.
+  return sqlite3_exec(db, "PRAGMA journal_mode=MEMORY; PRAGMA synchronous=OFF;", nullptr, nullptr, nullptr);
+#else
   // WAL mode + NORMAL = no need for disk syncs, consistent but not durable is fine.
   return sqlite3_exec(db, "PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL; PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL;", nullptr, nullptr, nullptr);
+#endif
 }
 
 // A result that means the file itself is damaged (a torn write, a truncated or overwritten file):
@@ -26,7 +32,14 @@ inline bool is_damaged(int rc) {
 }
 
 // Whether a cache that failed to open with this result is deleted and created once more.
-inline bool reset_on_open_failure(int rc) { return is_damaged(rc); }
+inline bool reset_on_open_failure(int rc) {
+#if defined(__SWITCH__)
+  // Horizon: any failure (the console's failures did not all say "malformed").
+  return rc != SQLITE_OK;
+#else
+  return is_damaged(rc);
+#endif
+}
 
 // Removes a cache database and the files sqlite keeps next to it, so the caller can start it again
 // from an empty file. Returns the size the database had, or -1.
